    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Documentation vs. Code:</strong> Students often mistakenly believe the IA is only about the code. The documentation determines the majority of the marks.</li>
            <li><strong>Innovation vs. Templates:</strong> Believing that using a pre-existing template (e.g., a simple web template) is acceptable. Innovation and modification must be explicitly documented.</li>
            <li><strong>Teacher Debugging:</strong> Expecting the teacher to write or debug the source code. Teachers must remain as advisors, not co-developers.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Ethics of Data:</strong> What is the developer's responsibility regarding the confidentiality and security of original data collected from stakeholders?</li>
                <li><strong>Knowledge Transformation:</strong> Does the process of translating a human problem into a code-based solution lose essential nuances of the original context?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

